@brief
A ticket.

    ORD-905  The regional order exports still land as raw rows. Convert
             the rows in all three files to the fixture format. Leave the
             BEGIN/EXPORT markers alone — the generator writes between
             them next month.

Three files, opened as the argument list. In each one, the rows between
`// BEGIN EXPORT` and `// END EXPORT` become object literals:

    ord_1001;cus_501;4200   →     { id: 'ord_1001', customerId: 'cus_501', total: 4200 },

The files have different numbers of rows, and nothing outside the markers
changes.

This is the chapter's last shape, and it is three ideas stacked. Record
the per-row edit once as a macro. Describe the rows with a pattern range,
so it fits whatever each file contains. Run that over the argument list
with `:argdo`, writing as it goes.

Give the macro a guard — start it with `0f;`. On a row that has already
been converted there is no semicolon left, the `f;` fails, and the rest
of the macro never runs. That is what makes the command safe to fire at
a file you have already half fixed by hand, which you are about to have.

@keys
| `qq` … `q` | record into register `q` |
| `:/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1normal @q` | play `q` on the rows between the markers |
| `:argdo execute "{cmd}" | update` | run it in every file on the argument list, writing the ones that change |
| `:normal` swallows `|` | which is why the ranged command goes inside `:execute` |
| `:qa` | quit once everything is written |

@hints
Record on the first row of the first file and let the recording convert
that row. The guard means `:argdo` can visit it again without harm.
--
The edit for one row: `0f;` then `s` to replace the first semicolon with
`', customerId: '`, then the second with `', total: `, then `0i` for the
opening `  { id: '` and `A` for the ` },`. Four small edits, one Esc
between each.
--
The range is the pattern pair with offsets, so the markers survive:
`/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1`. It is resolved fresh in each file, which is why the
files may differ in length.
--
`3G`, then `qq0f;s', customerId: '<Esc>f;s', total: <Esc>0i  { id: '<Esc>A },<Esc>q`,
then `:argdo execute "/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1normal @q" | update` Enter, then `:qa`.
The quotes are not decoration — see the solution.

@solution
    3G
    qq 0f; s', customerId: ' <Esc> f; s', total:  <Esc> 0i  { id: ' <Esc> A }, <Esc> q
    :argdo execute "/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1normal @q" | update<Enter>
    :qa<Enter>

A hundred and fourteen keystrokes for twelve rows in three files, and the same
command for a hundred and twenty rows in thirty files.

The `:execute` is doing something specific here. `:normal` takes
**everything** after it literally, and that includes a `|`. Written as
`:argdo /BEGIN/+1,/END/-1normal @q | update`, the keys handed to
`:normal` are `@q | update` — the macro, then `|`, then `u` for undo,
then `p` for put, and so on down the word. It does not fail cleanly; it
quietly does something else. Wrapping the ranged command in a string ends
it at the closing quote, so the `|` belongs to `:argdo` again and
`update` is a command rather than four keystrokes.

The rule from the last lesson generalises: when `:normal` is not the last
thing on the line, it has to be inside `:execute`.

Read the `:argdo` line from the inside out. `@q` is one row. `:normal @q`
is one row, done to each line of a range. `/BEGIN/+1,/END/-1` is "the
rows, wherever they are and however many". `:argdo` is "in every file on
the list". `| update` is "and write the ones that changed". Each layer
knows nothing about the others, which is why they compose at all.

Three decisions in there are worth keeping:

**The guard did the work of a condition.** `0f;` is not navigation, it is
a test — "does this line still need converting?". A macro that fails
harmlessly on lines that are already done can be run over anything,
including its own output. Without it you would have had to exclude the
first file, or undo the recording, or count rows.

**The range described the block instead of pointing at it.** Line numbers
would have needed three different commands, one per file. `:argdo` takes
one command, so the range had to be one that means the right thing in all
three — which is exactly what pattern addresses are for.

**`| update`, not `| w`.** The files that did not change keep their
timestamps. On three files nobody notices; on a repo-wide `:argdo` it is
the difference between a quiet `git status` and a hundred files touched.

If the rows ever stop being semicolon-separated, none of this survives —
but you will not have written a script you now have to maintain, either.
That is the trade the whole tool makes: a command you compose in ten
seconds, use once, and throw away.

@start orders.eu.ts
export const euOrders = [
// BEGIN EXPORT
ord_1001;cus_501;4200
ord_1002;cus_502;1899
ord_1003;cus_501;650
ord_1004;cus_503;12750
// END EXPORT
];

@expected orders.eu.ts
export const euOrders = [
// BEGIN EXPORT
  { id: 'ord_1001', customerId: 'cus_501', total: 4200 },
  { id: 'ord_1002', customerId: 'cus_502', total: 1899 },
  { id: 'ord_1003', customerId: 'cus_501', total: 650 },
  { id: 'ord_1004', customerId: 'cus_503', total: 12750 },
// END EXPORT
];

